// Note bodies. i = defining identity, t = text; {{Wiki title|text}} and [[id|text]] are links.
const BODY = {
pvsnp:{i:"one-way functions exist ⟹ P ≠ NP",
t:"Cryptography needs {{P versus NP problem|P ≠ NP}}: inverting a [[owf|one-way function]] is an {{NP (complexity)|NP}} search problem, so a fast algorithm for NP would break every scheme at once. The converse is the hard direction. Worst-case NP-hardness is not known to give anything usable, because cryptography needs problems that are hard on [[avgcase|random instances]], not only on the worst one.\n\nThere are barriers, too. Brassard observed that if inverting a one-way permutation were NP-hard, NP would collapse into {{Co-NP|coNP}}, which almost everyone believes false."},
avgcase:{i:"Algorithmica · Heuristica · Pessiland · Minicrypt · Cryptomania",
t:"Cryptography lives on random instances drawn from an efficiently samplable distribution, and it needs instances that come with solutions planted in them. {{Average-case complexity}} is therefore the real substrate. {{Leonid Levin}} built its theory of distributional NP, and {{Russell Impagliazzo}} framed the possibilities as five worlds, running from Algorithmica, where P = NP, to Cryptomania, where public-key cryptography exists.\n\nThe gap between the worlds is the gap between worst and average case. Bogdanov and Trevisan showed that non-adaptive reductions from worst-case NP-hardness to average-case hardness would collapse the {{Polynomial hierarchy|polynomial hierarchy}}, so most assumptions are bets placed directly on this layer. [[lattices|Lattices]] are the rare exception, with provable worst-to-average reductions, and [[dlog|discrete log]] is {{Random self-reducibility|random self-reducible}}, which makes every instance in a group equally hard."},
finegrained:{i:"honest cost <i>n</i>,   attack cost ≈ <i>n</i><sup>2</sup>   (Merkle puzzles)",
t:"Ordinary cryptography wants honest users to run in polynomial time and attackers to need superpolynomial time. Fine-grained cryptography accepts a smaller gap. Merkle's puzzles were the first example: Alice and Bob each make about n queries to a random function and end up with a shared key, and an eavesdropper needs about n² queries to find it. Barak and Mahmoody proved that no key exchange built only from a [[rom|random oracle]] can do better than this quadratic gap.\n\nNewer work takes its hardness from fine-grained complexity, from problems like orthogonal vectors, 3SUM and k-clique whose best known algorithms are tied to the {{Exponential time hypothesis|strong exponential time hypothesis}}. Ball, Rosen, Sabin and Vasudevan built proofs of work this way, and LaVigne, Lincoln and Vassilevska Williams built public-key encryption with a fine-grained gap.\n\nAnother version limits the attacker instead of the gap. Degwekar, Vaikuntanathan and Vasudevan built primitives that no {{AC0}} circuit can break, with no assumption at all, and primitives secure against {{NC (complexity)|NC¹}} from a mild [[circuits|circuit-complexity]] separation. Part of the appeal is insurance: these schemes might still work in one of [[avgcase|Impagliazzo's worlds]] where [[owf|one-way functions]] do not exist."},
metac:{i:"one-way functions exist ⟺ K<sup><i>t</i></sup> is mildly hard on average",
t:"Meta-complexity asks how hard it is to compute complexity itself: the {{Minimum circuit size problem|minimum circuit size problem}}, or time-bounded {{Kolmogorov complexity}}. Liu and Pass showed that [[owf|one-way functions]] exist exactly when time-bounded Kolmogorov complexity is mildly hard on average, giving Minicrypt its first natural complete problem.\n\nThe area also suggests a route around the [[avgcase|worst-to-average barrier]]. Hirahara gave non-black-box worst-case to average-case reductions for problems about Kolmogorov complexity, of a kind that black-box techniques cannot provide for NP-complete problems."},
natural:{i:"PRFs in <i>C</i>  ⟹  no natural property is useful against <i>C</i>",
t:"Razborov and Rudich noticed that almost every known [[circuits|circuit lower bound]] has the same shape. The proof finds a property of Boolean functions that can be checked quickly from the truth table, that a large fraction of all functions have, and that no function with small circuits has. They called these proofs natural.\n\nA natural proof can be turned into an attack. Give it the truth table of a function and check the property: a random function has it fairly often, and a function with small circuits never does. That distinguishes the two, so if [[prf|pseudorandom functions]] can be computed in some circuit class, there is no natural proof of lower bounds against that class. Naor and Reingold's PRFs are believed secure and are computable in TC⁰, which is roughly why lower bounds have stalled near there.\n\nThe connection also goes the other way. Carmosino, Impagliazzo, Kabanets and Kolokolova turned natural properties into [[learning]] algorithms, and asking whether a truth table has small circuits is exactly the minimum circuit size problem studied in [[metac|meta-complexity]]."},
owf:{i:"Pr<sub><i>x</i>←{0,1}<sup><i>n</i></sup></sub>[ <i>f</i>(<i>A</i>(<i>f</i>(<i>x</i>))) = <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) ] ≤ negl(<i>n</i>)",
t:"A {{One-way function|one-way function}} is easy to compute and hard to invert on a random input. It is the minimal assumption of complexity-based cryptography. Impagliazzo and Luby showed that almost every primitive implies one, and conversely one-way functions give [[prg|pseudorandom generators]], [[prf|pseudorandom functions]], [[mac|MACs]], [[sigs|signatures]] and [[commit|commitments]]. Impagliazzo calls the world where they exist, but nothing stronger does, Minicrypt.\n\nWeak one-way functions, hard to invert only a noticeable fraction of the time, become strong ones when many independent copies are taken together, an argument of Yao in the same family as {{Yao's XOR lemma}}. Natural candidates are multiplying primes ([[factoring]]) and modular exponentiation ([[dlog|discrete log]])."},
factoring:{i:"<i>N</i> = <i>p</i>·<i>q</i> ↦ find <i>p</i>, <i>q</i>    ·    <i>x</i> ↦ <i>x</i><sup><i>e</i></sup> mod <i>N</i>",
t:"{{Integer factorization}} of a product of two large primes has no known classical polynomial-time algorithm; the best is the {{General number field sieve|general number field sieve}}. The {{RSA (cryptosystem)|RSA}} function x ↦ x<sup>e</sup> mod N is a [[tdf|trapdoor permutation]] for anyone who knows the factors, and squaring mod N, behind the {{Rabin cryptosystem}}, is provably as hard to invert as factoring.\n\nThe {{Quadratic residuosity problem|quadratic residuosity problem}} gave the first semantically secure encryption, the {{Goldwasser–Micali cryptosystem}}, and later single-server [[pir|private information retrieval]]. All of it falls to {{Shor's algorithm}} on a large [[quantcomp|quantum computer]]."},
dlog:{i:"(<i>g</i><sup><i>a</i></sup>, <i>g</i><sup><i>b</i></sup>, <i>g</i><sup><i>ab</i></sup>) ≈<sub>c</sub> (<i>g</i><sup><i>a</i></sup>, <i>g</i><sup><i>b</i></sup>, <i>g</i><sup><i>c</i></sup>)",
t:"In a cyclic group of prime order, recovering a from g<sup>a</sup> is the {{Discrete logarithm|discrete logarithm}} problem, computing g<sup>ab</sup> from g<sup>a</sup> and g<sup>b</sup> is the {{Computational Diffie–Hellman assumption|computational Diffie–Hellman}} problem, and telling g<sup>ab</sup> apart from random is {{Decisional Diffie–Hellman assumption|decisional Diffie–Hellman}}, shown above. In the {{Generic group model|generic group model}} Shoup proved that discrete log takes about √p operations, which is why {{Elliptic-curve cryptography|elliptic-curve groups}}, with no better known attacks, are used in practice.\n\nThe homomorphic structure makes these groups the workhorse for [[sigma|sigma protocols]], {{ElGamal encryption}}, the {{Cramer–Shoup cryptosystem}} and the {{Naor–Reingold pseudorandom function}}. {{Shor's algorithm}} breaks all of them."},
pairings:{i:"<i>e</i>(<i>g</i><sup><i>a</i></sup>, <i>g</i><sup><i>b</i></sup>) = <i>e</i>(<i>g</i>, <i>g</i>)<sup><i>ab</i></sup>",
t:"A bilinear pairing maps two group elements into a target group so that exponents multiply. That makes [[dlog|DDH]] easy in the source group, but new assumptions take its place and unlock a remarkable amount: Joux's one-round three-party key exchange, the {{Boneh–Franklin scheme}} for [[ibe|identity-based encryption]], short {{BLS digital signature|BLS signatures}}, perfect [[nizk|NIZK]] proofs, and pairing-based [[snark|SNARKs]] such as Groth16.\n\n{{Pairing-based cryptography}} covers the constructions and the curves used in practice. Together with [[lpn|LPN]] and local pseudorandom generators, pairings are also one of the three ingredients of Jain–Lin–Sahai [[io|obfuscation]]."},
lattices:{i:"(<b>A</b>, <b>A</b><i>s</i> + <i>e</i>) ≈<sub>c</sub> (<b>A</b>, <i>u</i>)",
t:"Two problems carry {{Lattice-based cryptography|lattice-based cryptography}}. The {{Short integer solution problem|short integer solution}} problem asks for a short nonzero z with Az = 0 mod q; {{Learning with errors|learning with errors}} asks to recover s from noisy linear equations, or merely to tell them from random, as above. Ajtai proved that average-case SIS is as hard as approximating worst-case [[geometry|lattice problems]], and Regev did the same for LWE through a quantum reduction, later made classical for some parameters by Peikert.\n\nNo efficient quantum attack is known, so lattices are the main family of {{Post-quantum cryptography|post-quantum cryptography}}. [[rlwe|Ring and module variants]] make them efficient, and {{Kyber}} is now standardized as ML-KEM. LWE is also expressive enough to build [[fhe|fully homomorphic encryption]], [[ibe|attribute-based encryption]] for circuits, [[nizk|NIZKs]], [[snarg|SNARGs for P]] and [[folding|folding schemes]]."},
rlwe:{i:"(<i>a</i>, <i>a</i>·<i>s</i> + <i>e</i>) ≈<sub>c</sub> (<i>a</i>, <i>u</i>)   in <i>R</i><sub><i>q</i></sub> = ℤ<sub><i>q</i></sub>[<i>X</i>]/(<i>X</i><sup><i>n</i></sup> + 1)",
t:"Plain [[lattices|LWE]] needs a random matrix with n² entries, which makes keys large. {{Ring learning with errors|Ring-LWE}} replaces the matrix with multiplication by one random element of a polynomial ring such as ℤ<sub>q</sub>[X]/(X<sup>n</sup> + 1), so a single ring element carries n correlated samples and products cost O(n log n) through the {{Discrete Fourier transform over a ring|number-theoretic transform}}. Lyubashevsky, Peikert and Regev proved Ring-LWE as hard as worst-case problems on {{Ideal lattice|ideal lattices}}, through a quantum reduction. Module-LWE works with small k × k matrices over the ring and interpolates between the two; Langlois and Stehlé gave its worst-case reduction.\n\nModule lattices carry the NIST post-quantum standards. {{Kyber}} became ML-KEM, an encryption scheme lifted to [[cca|CCA security]] by the Fujisaki–Okamoto transform, and Dilithium became ML-DSA, a Fiat–Shamir [[sigs|signature]] that uses Lyubashevsky's rejection sampling so that signatures leak nothing about the key. Packing many plaintext slots into one ring element is also what makes BGV, BFV and CKKS [[fhe|homomorphic encryption]] practical, and Ring-LWE gives [[pir|doubly efficient PIR]].\n\nThe extra structure is a calculated risk. Quantum attacks on ideal-lattice problems with very large approximation factors, due to Cramer, Ducas and Wesolowski, do not reach the parameters that Ring-LWE and Module-LWE need."},
lpn:{i:"(<b>A</b>, <b>A</b><i>s</i> + <i>e</i> mod 2) ≈<sub>c</sub> (<b>A</b>, <i>u</i>),   <i>e</i> ∼ Ber(<i>τ</i>)<sup><i>m</i></sup>",
t:"Learning parity with noise is [[lattices|LWE]] over the binary field: recover a secret from linear equations mod 2, each flipped with small probability. It is the decoding problem for random {{Linear code|linear codes}}, studied in [[coding|coding theory]] long before cryptography, and a natural problem in [[learning|learning theory]], where it is known as noisy {{Parity learning|parity learning}}.\n\nAt very low noise, Alekhnovich showed it yields [[pke|public-key encryption]]. The much older {{McEliece cryptosystem}} hides a {{Goppa code}} behind a random-looking generator matrix and remains a post-quantum candidate. Variants over large fields are one of the assumptions behind Jain–Lin–Sahai [[io|obfuscation]]."},
isogeny:{i:"given supersingular <i>E</i>, <i>E</i>′, find an isogeny <i>φ</i>: <i>E</i> → <i>E</i>′",
t:"Isogeny-based cryptography rests on the difficulty of finding a structure-preserving map between two supersingular elliptic curves. {{Supersingular isogeny key exchange|SIDH}} was a leading post-quantum candidate until Castryck and Decru broke it classically in 2022, exploiting the extra torsion-point information the protocol revealed.\n\nSchemes built on commutative group actions, such as CSIDH, reveal no such information and survive, though known quantum attacks push their parameters larger than first hoped."},
rom:{i:"<i>H</i> ← a uniformly random function, available only as an oracle",
t:"The {{Random oracle|random oracle model}} pretends that a hash function is a truly random function that everyone queries as a black box. Proofs may watch and even program the adversary's queries, which gives clean security arguments for practical schemes, including [[sigma|Fiat–Shamir]] signatures and the Fujisaki–Okamoto transform for [[cca|CCA security]].\n\nIt is a heuristic rather than an assumption: Canetti, Goldreich and Halevi built schemes that are secure in the model but insecure with every concrete hash function. Against [[quantadv|quantum adversaries]] the oracle must be queried in superposition, which breaks the usual proof tricks; Zhandry's compressed-oracle technique repairs many of them in the [[qrom|quantum random oracle model]]."},
otp:{i:"Pr[Enc(<i>k</i>, <i>m</i><sub>0</sub>) = <i>c</i>] = Pr[Enc(<i>k</i>, <i>m</i><sub>1</sub>) = <i>c</i>]",
t:"Perfect secrecy, the strongest form of {{Information-theoretic security|information-theoretic security}}, means the ciphertext carries no information at all about the message. The {{One-time pad|one-time pad}}, c = m ⊕ k, achieves it, and Shannon proved in {{Communication Theory of Secrecy Systems}} that it is essentially optimal: the key space must be at least as large as the message space.\n\nRelaxing to small {{Total variation distance of probability measures|statistical distance}} barely helps, so beating Shannon's bound requires restricting the adversary to efficient computation. That move, from perfect to [[ske|computational security]], is the founding step of modern cryptography."},
hashing:{i:"Pr<sub><i>h</i></sub>[<i>h</i>(<i>x</i><sub>1</sub>) = <i>y</i><sub>1</sub>, …, <i>h</i>(<i>x</i><sub><i>t</i></sub>) = <i>y</i><sub><i>t</i></sub>] = |<i>V</i>|<sup>−<i>t</i></sup>",
t:"A {{K-independent hashing|t-wise independent}} hash family makes its values at any t distinct points uniform and independent. A random polynomial of degree t − 1 over a finite field does the job, the same idea as [[ss|Shamir sharing]]; {{Universal hashing|universal hashing}} is Carter and Wegman's weaker pairwise notion.\n\nThese families give information-theoretic [[mac|MACs]]: a pairwise-independent hash authenticates one message, and a t-wise independent one authenticates t − 1. They also give the {{Leftover hash lemma|leftover hash lemma}} for [[extract|randomness extraction]], and they appear in countless derandomization arguments."},
ss:{i:"<i>p</i>(<i>x</i>) = <i>s</i> + <i>a</i><sub>1</sub><i>x</i> + ⋯ + <i>a</i><sub><i>t</i></sub><i>x</i><sup><i>t</i></sup>,   shares <i>p</i>(1), …, <i>p</i>(<i>n</i>)",
t:"{{Secret sharing}} splits a secret so that any t + 1 shares reconstruct it and any t reveal nothing. {{Shamir's secret sharing|Shamir's scheme}} hides the secret in the constant term of a random polynomial of degree t. Reconstruction is Lagrange interpolation, and the shares form a {{Reed–Solomon error correction|Reed–Solomon}} codeword, so even corrupted shares can be corrected.\n\nLinear schemes for general access structures correspond to monotone span programs (Karchmer and Wigderson). {{Verifiable secret sharing}} protects against a dishonest dealer, and computing on shares is the basis of [[mpcit|honest-majority MPC]] and [[threshold|threshold cryptography]]."},
mpcit:{i:"perfect security for <i>t</i> &lt; <i>n</i>/3   ·   statistical security for <i>t</i> &lt; <i>n</i>/2 with broadcast",
t:"With an honest majority, {{Secure multi-party computation|secure multi-party computation}} needs no computational assumptions at all. In the protocols of Ben-Or, Goldwasser and Wigderson and of Chaum, Crépeau and Damgård, parties [[ss|secret-share]] their inputs and evaluate the circuit gate by gate on the shares: additions are local, and multiplications use a degree-reduction step. Perfect security holds against fewer than n/3 corruptions; with a broadcast channel and {{Verifiable secret sharing|verifiable secret sharing}}, Rabin and Ben-Or reached n/2 with statistical security.\n\nBeyond those thresholds, [[mpc|computational assumptions]] such as [[ot|oblivious transfer]] become unavoidable."},
extract:{i:"<i>H</i><sub>∞</sub>(<i>X</i>) ≥ <i>k</i>  ⟹  Ext(<i>X</i>, <i>S</i>) ≈<sub><i>ε</i></sub> <i>U</i><sub><i>m</i></sub>,   <i>m</i> = <i>k</i> − 2 log(1/<i>ε</i>)",
t:"A {{Randomness extractor|randomness extractor}} turns a weak source with high {{Min-entropy|min-entropy}} into nearly uniform bits, using a short random seed. The {{Leftover hash lemma|leftover hash lemma}} says a [[hashing|universal hash]] already does this while losing only 2 log(1/ε) bits, and it is a key step in building [[prg|pseudorandom generators]] from any [[owf|one-way function]].\n\nNisan and Zuckerman used extractors to run randomized space-bounded algorithms with few truly random bits, and {{Fuzzy extractor|fuzzy extractors}} reliably derive keys from noisy secrets such as biometrics. The same lemma drives [[privamp|privacy amplification]]."},
privamp:{i:"<i>H</i><sub>∞</sub>(<i>X</i> | <i>E</i>) ≥ <i>k</i>  ⟹  (<i>h</i>, <i>h</i>(<i>X</i>), <i>E</i>) ≈<sub><i>ε</i></sub> (<i>h</i>, <i>U</i><sub><i>m</i></sub>, <i>E</i>)   for <i>m</i> = <i>k</i> − 2 log(1/<i>ε</i>)",
t:"Say Alice and Bob share a string X, and Eve knows part of it, so that X still has k bits of min-entropy from her point of view. To get a clean key, they pick a [[hashing|universal hash function]] at random, announce it in public, and hash X. The {{Leftover hash lemma|leftover hash lemma}} says the result looks uniform to Eve, even though she sees the hash function, as long as the output is about 2 log(1/ε) bits shorter than k. In the language of [[extract|extractors]], universal hashing is a strong extractor.\n\nThe partial secret has to come from somewhere. Wyner showed that if Eve's channel is noisier than Bob's, Alice can send Bob bits that Eve only partly learns. Maurer showed that back-and-forth public discussion can produce a key even when Eve's channel is better, as long as the noise on the three channels is not perfectly correlated. In [[qcrypto|quantum key distribution]], physics limits what Eve can learn, and privacy amplification is the final step of the protocol.\n\nThis all assumes Eve only listens. If she can change the public messages, the hash function itself has to be authenticated using nothing but the weak secret, and Dodis and Wichs did this with non-malleable extractors."},
prg:{i:"<i>G</i>(<i>U</i><sub><i>n</i></sub>) ≈<sub>c</sub> <i>U</i><sub><i>n</i>+<i>ℓ</i></sub>",
t:"A cryptographic {{Pseudorandom generator|pseudorandom generator}} stretches a short random seed into a longer string that no efficient test can tell from uniform. Blum and Micali, and independently Yao, built one from a [[hardcore|hardcore bit]] of a one-way permutation (the {{Blum–Micali algorithm}}); Håstad, Impagliazzo, Levin and Luby built one from any [[owf|one-way function]], so the two are equivalent.\n\nOne bit of stretch can be iterated to any polynomial stretch, a classic use of the [[reduce|hybrid argument]]. The GGM tree turns a PRG into a [[prf|pseudorandom function]], and Naor used one for [[commit|commitments]]. The generators of [[derand|derandomization]] share the definition but fool only circuits of a fixed size, trading one-way hardness for circuit lower bounds."},
hardcore:{i:"(<i>f</i>(<i>x</i>), <i>r</i>, ⟨<i>x</i>, <i>r</i>⟩) ≈<sub>c</sub> (<i>f</i>(<i>x</i>), <i>r</i>, <i>U</i><sub>1</sub>)",
t:"A {{Hard-core predicate|hardcore predicate}} is a bit of the input that stays unpredictable given f(x). Goldreich and Levin showed that every [[owf|one-way function]] has one after a small modification: the inner product of x with a random string r. Their proof is a local {{List decoding|list-decoding}} algorithm for the {{Hadamard code}}, an early meeting point of cryptography and [[coding|coding theory]].\n\nSpecific functions have natural hardcore bits too, such as the least significant bit of {{RSA (cryptosystem)|RSA}} and of Rabin's function (Alexi, Chor, Goldreich and Schnorr). Hardcore bits are exactly what turns a one-way permutation into a [[prg|pseudorandom generator]]."},
prf:{i:"<i>F</i><sub><i>k</i></sub>(<i>x</i><sub>1</sub>⋯<i>x</i><sub><i>n</i></sub>) = <i>G</i><sub><i>x</i><sub><i>n</i></sub></sub>(⋯ <i>G</i><sub><i>x</i><sub>1</sub></sub>(<i>k</i>) ⋯)",
t:"A {{Pseudorandom function family|pseudorandom function}} is a keyed function that no efficient algorithm with oracle access can tell from a truly random one. Goldreich, Goldwasser and Micali built one from any length-doubling [[prg|PRG]] by walking down a binary tree, keeping the left or right half of the output at each input bit, as the identity above shows.\n\nPRFs give [[ske|CPA-secure encryption]] and [[mac|MACs]] directly, and three {{Feistel cipher|Feistel}} rounds turn them into [[prp|permutations]]. The {{Naor–Reingold pseudorandom function}} from [[dlog|DDH]] and the construction of Banerjee, Peikert and Rosen from [[lattices|LWE]] have low depth, which is exactly why PRFs block [[circuits|natural proofs]] of circuit lower bounds."},
prp:{i:"(<i>L</i>, <i>R</i>) ↦ (<i>R</i>, <i>L</i> ⊕ <i>F</i><sub><i>k</i></sub>(<i>R</i>))",
t:"A {{Pseudorandom permutation|pseudorandom permutation}} is an efficiently invertible keyed permutation that looks like a random one; it is the theoretical model of a {{Block cipher|block cipher}}. Luby and Rackoff proved that three rounds of the {{Feistel cipher|Feistel}} construction, each XORing a [[prf|PRF]] of one half into the other, give a PRP, and four rounds give a strong PRP, secure even when the adversary can also query the inverse."},
ske:{i:"Enc(<i>k</i>, <i>m</i>) = (<i>r</i>, <i>F</i><sub><i>k</i></sub>(<i>r</i>) ⊕ <i>m</i>)",
t:"Goldwasser and Micali defined {{Semantic security|semantic security}}, that a ciphertext reveals nothing efficiently computable about the message, and showed it equivalent to {{Ciphertext indistinguishability|indistinguishability}} of the encryptions of any two messages. Encrypting many messages under one key needs randomness or state, and the right notion becomes security against {{Chosen-plaintext attack|chosen-plaintext attacks}}.\n\nA [[prf|PRF]] applied to a fresh random nonce, as above, gives CPA security. Adding a [[mac|MAC]] over the ciphertext gives {{Authenticated encryption|authenticated encryption}}, which is also [[cca|CCA-secure]]."},
mac:{i:"<i>t</i> = <i>F</i><sub><i>k</i></sub>(<i>m</i>),   and no efficient forger finds a valid <i>t</i>* for a new <i>m</i>*",
t:"A {{Message authentication code|message authentication code}} lets two parties who share a key detect tampering: no efficient adversary, even after seeing tags on messages of its choice, can produce a valid tag on a new message. Any [[prf|PRF]] is a MAC for fixed-length messages, and domain-extension constructions such as {{CBC-MAC}} and {{HMAC}} handle long ones.\n\nWithout computational assumptions, [[hashing|pairwise-independent hashing]] gives one-time MACs in the style of Wegman and Carter, and t-wise independence authenticates up to t − 1 messages."},
crhf:{i:"Pr[<i>A</i>(<i>h</i>) → <i>x</i> ≠ <i>x</i>′ with <i>h</i>(<i>x</i>) = <i>h</i>(<i>x</i>′)] ≤ negl",
t:"A hash function is {{Collision resistance|collision-resistant}} if no efficient algorithm can find two inputs with the same output. The {{Merkle–Damgård construction}} extends a fixed-length compression function to inputs of any length, and {{Merkle tree|Merkle trees}} commit to long vectors with short openings, the backbone of [[snark|succinct arguments]] and hash-based [[sigs|signatures]].\n\nUnlike most symmetric primitives, collision resistance is not known to follow from [[owf|one-way functions]], and Simon proved that it cannot follow by a [[blackbox|black-box construction]]. Concrete constructions come from [[dlog|discrete log]], [[factoring]] and [[lattices|SIS]]."},
commit:{i:"Com(<i>m</i>; <i>r</i>) = <i>g</i><sup><i>m</i></sup><i>h</i><sup><i>r</i></sup>   (Pedersen)",
t:"A {{Commitment scheme|commitment scheme}} is a digital sealed envelope: the committer is bound to a value, but the receiver learns nothing until it is opened. Binding and hiding cannot both be perfect. Naor's scheme from any [[prg|PRG]] is statistically binding, Pedersen's commitment from [[dlog|discrete log]] is perfectly hiding, and Haitner and coauthors showed statistically hiding commitments also follow from any [[owf|one-way function]].\n\nCommitments drive [[zk|zero-knowledge proofs]] for NP, coin flipping, and the polynomial and vector commitments inside modern [[snark|SNARKs]]."},
sigs:{i:"σ(<i>m</i>) = (<i>x</i><sub>1,<i>m</i><sub>1</sub></sub>, …, <i>x</i><sub><i>n</i>,<i>m</i><sub><i>n</i></sub></sub>),   pk = {<i>f</i>(<i>x</i><sub><i>i</i>,<i>b</i></sub>)}   (Lamport)",
t:"{{Digital signature|Digital signatures}} let anyone check, with a public key, that a message came from the holder of the secret key. Surprisingly, they need only [[owf|one-way functions]]. {{Lamport signature|Lamport's one-time signature}} reveals one preimage per message bit, and tree constructions in the style of the {{Merkle signature scheme}} turn one-time schemes into many-time ones. Naor and Yung reduced the problem to {{Universal one-way hash function|universal one-way hash functions}}, and Rompel built those from any one-way function.\n\nSo signatures live in Minicrypt even though they are public-key objects. Goldwasser, Micali and Rivest gave the modern definition, security against adaptive chosen-message attacks. In practice, {{Schnorr signature|Schnorr signatures}} come from [[sigma|sigma protocols]] via Fiat–Shamir, and post-quantum [[lattices|lattice]] signatures use either trapdoor sampling (Falcon) or Fiat–Shamir with rejection sampling ([[rlwe|ML-DSA]])."},
threshold:{i:"σ = ∏<sub><i>i</i>∈<i>S</i></sub> σ<sub><i>i</i></sub><sup>λ<sub><i>i</i></sub></sup>   for any set <i>S</i> of <i>t</i> signers",
t:"A secret key in one place is a single point of failure. Threshold cryptography spreads it out: the key is split with [[ss|Shamir secret sharing]] among n parties, any t of them can sign or decrypt together, and any t − 1 learn nothing about it. The key is never rebuilt. Each party computes a partial result from its own share, and the partial results are combined with Lagrange coefficients λ<sub>i</sub>, as above.\n\nHow hard this is depends on the scheme. BLS [[sigs|signatures]], which use [[pairings|bilinear maps]], are linear in the secret key, so each server signs on its own and the pieces combine with no interaction. RSA is harder because the group order has to stay secret, and Shoup's scheme in 2000 was the first practical one. Schnorr signatures need the signers to agree on shared randomness first, and FROST does this in two rounds. Threshold decryption works the same way for [[pke|public-key encryption]].\n\nIf a trusted dealer handed out the shares, that dealer would be the new single point of failure. Distributed key generation lets the parties create the shared key themselves; Pedersen proposed a protocol and Gennaro, Jarecki, Krawczyk and Rabin fixed a flaw in it. Seen from a distance, threshold cryptography is [[mpc|secure computation]] for one specific function, and that narrow focus is what makes it fast."},
pke:{i:"Enc(pk, <i>m</i><sub>0</sub>) ≈<sub>c</sub> Enc(pk, <i>m</i><sub>1</sub>)   given pk",
t:"{{Public-key cryptography|Public-key encryption}} lets anyone encrypt to a published key. It lives in Cryptomania: Impagliazzo and Rudich showed that key agreement cannot be built from [[owf|one-way functions]] in a [[blackbox|black-box way]], so every scheme rests on structured assumptions.\n\nThe classical routes are [[kex|Diffie–Hellman]] and {{ElGamal encryption|ElGamal}} from [[dlog|discrete log]], RSA and Goldwasser–Micali from [[factoring]], and Regev's scheme from [[lattices|LWE]]. Real systems package these as a {{Key encapsulation mechanism|key encapsulation mechanism}} feeding a symmetric cipher, and they need [[cca|CCA security]]. [[io|Indistinguishability obfuscation]] plus any one-way function also yields public-key encryption."},
kex:{i:"<i>g</i><sup><i>a</i></sup>,  <i>g</i><sup><i>b</i></sup>   ⟶   <i>K</i> = <i>g</i><sup><i>ab</i></sup>",
t:"In {{Diffie–Hellman key exchange}}, Alice sends g<sup>a</sup>, Bob sends g<sup>b</sup>, and each computes g<sup>ab</sup>. An eavesdropper sees only the two messages and has to solve the computational Diffie–Hellman problem in a [[dlog|discrete-log group]]. Public-key cryptography starts with this protocol. A key exchange with two messages is really the same thing as [[pke|public-key encryption]]: the first message acts as a public key, and the second encrypts the shared key under it.\n\nKey exchange is also where [[blackbox|black-box separations]] first showed up. Impagliazzo and Rudich proved that it cannot be built from a random permutation in a black-box way, which is why it sits in Cryptomania and not Minicrypt. With only a random oracle, [[finegrained|Merkle's puzzles]] and their quadratic gap are the best possible.\n\nA real protocol also has to stop an active attacker who sits in the middle and talks to each side. Authenticated key exchange binds the messages to long-term keys or [[sigs|signatures]]. Its security models, starting with Bellare and Rogaway and later Canetti and Krawczyk, ask that each session key look random even if other sessions leak, and that stealing a long-term key later should not expose past sessions, a property called forward secrecy. TLS 1.3 follows this design."},
tdf:{i:"<i>x</i> ↦ <i>f</i><sub>pk</sub>(<i>x</i>) is easy;   <i>f</i><sub>pk</sub><sup>−1</sup> is easy with sk and hard without",
t:"A {{Trapdoor function|trapdoor function}} is a one-way function that becomes easy to invert given secret information. RSA and Rabin's function are the classical examples. A [[hardcore|hardcore bit]] of a trapdoor permutation gives [[pke|public-key encryption]], and Even, Goldreich and Lempel built [[ot|oblivious transfer]] from them.\n\nPeikert and Waters introduced lossy trapdoor functions, which yield [[cca|CCA security]]. On [[lattices]], a short basis is a trapdoor: Gentry, Peikert and Vaikuntanathan showed how to sample short preimages with it, giving hash-and-sign signatures and lattice [[ibe|identity-based encryption]]."},
cca:{i:"Enc(pk, <i>m</i><sub>0</sub>) ≈<sub>c</sub> Enc(pk, <i>m</i><sub>1</sub>)   even given Dec(sk, ·) on every <i>c</i> ≠ <i>c</i>*",
t:"{{Chosen-ciphertext attack|Chosen-ciphertext security}} keeps encryptions indistinguishable even when the adversary can decrypt any other ciphertext. It is the right notion for real systems, and it requires non-{{Malleability (cryptography)|malleability}}: no way to maul a ciphertext into one of a related message.\n\nNaor and Yung, and then Dolev, Dwork and Naor, achieved it with [[nizk|NIZK]] proofs. The {{Cramer–Shoup cryptosystem}} was the first practical scheme, from [[dlog|DDH]], and lossy [[tdf|trapdoor functions]] give another route. In the [[rom|random oracle model]] the Fujisaki–Okamoto transform upgrades weaker schemes; a variant of it sits inside ML-KEM."},
ot:{i:"sender (<i>m</i><sub>0</sub>, <i>m</i><sub>1</sub>), receiver <i>b</i>  ⟶  receiver learns <i>m</i><sub><i>b</i></sub> and nothing else",
t:"In {{Oblivious transfer|oblivious transfer}} the receiver obtains one of the sender's two messages, and neither side learns more: the sender does not learn which one was taken, and the receiver does not learn the other. Rabin introduced a variant, and Even, Goldreich and Lempel built 1-out-of-2 OT from [[tdf|trapdoor permutations]].\n\nKilian proved that OT is complete: it suffices for [[mpc|secure computation]] of any function. OT extension, due to Ishai, Kilian, Nissim and Petrank, turns a few public-key OTs into millions of cheap ones using only symmetric primitives, which is what makes [[garbled|garbled-circuit]] protocols practical."},
ibe:{i:"sk<sub>id</sub> ← KeyGen(msk, id),   Dec(sk<sub>id</sub>, Enc(mpk, id, <i>m</i>)) = <i>m</i>",
t:"In {{Identity-based encryption|identity-based encryption}} any string, such as an email address, can serve as a public key, and an authority holding a master secret issues the matching secret keys. Shamir posed the problem in 1984. It was solved in 2001 by the {{Boneh–Franklin scheme}} from [[pairings]] and by Cocks from quadratic residues, and Gentry, Peikert and Vaikuntanathan later gave a lattice scheme using [[tdf|trapdoor sampling]].\n\n{{Attribute-based encryption}} generalizes it to access policies (Sahai and Waters, then Goyal, Pandey, Sahai and Waters), and Gorbunov, Vaikuntanathan and Wee handled policies given by any circuit, from [[lattices|LWE]]. Both are special cases of [[fe|functional encryption]]."},
reduce:{i:"|<i>p</i><sub>0</sub> − <i>p</i><sub><i>q</i></sub>| ≤ Σ<sub><i>i</i></sub> |<i>p</i><sub><i>i</i></sub> − <i>p</i><sub><i>i</i>+1</sub>|",
t:"{{Provable security}} turns any adversary that breaks a scheme into an algorithm that solves a hard problem. The basic tool is the hybrid argument: to show two distributions are {{Computational indistinguishability|computationally indistinguishable}}, walk from one to the other through intermediate hybrids that differ in one place each, and charge the total distinguishing advantage to the individual steps, as in the triangle inequality above.\n\nShoup, and Bellare and Rogaway, systematized this as game-hopping proofs. Concrete security tracks how much {{Advantage (cryptography)|advantage}} and running time a reduction loses, and whether it is tight, which decides real key sizes. A [[blackbox|black-box reduction]] uses the adversary only as an oracle, and most proofs are of this kind."},
simul:{i:"View<sub><i>A</i></sub>(real execution) ≈ Sim(what <i>A</i> is entitled to learn)",
t:"The simulation paradigm defines security by asking that everything an adversary sees could have been produced by a simulator that knows only what the adversary is entitled to learn. Goldwasser, Micali and Rackoff introduced it to define [[zk|zero-knowledge]], and it became the real/ideal paradigm of [[mpc|secure computation]], in which the real protocol must be indistinguishable from an ideal world with a trusted party.\n\n[[uc|Universal composability]] extends the idea to protocols running alongside others, and [[rewind|rewinding]] is how most simulators are built. Lindell's tutorial How to Simulate It is the standard practical guide."},
uc:{i:"REAL<sub><i>π</i>, <i>A</i>, <i>Z</i></sub> ≈ IDEAL<sub>ℱ, <i>S</i>, <i>Z</i></sub>   for every environment <i>Z</i>",
t:"{{Universal composability}}, due to Canetti, asks that a protocol remain secure when it runs concurrently with arbitrary other protocols. An environment that orchestrates everything must be unable to tell the real protocol from an ideal functionality ℱ paired with a simulator, and a composition theorem then lets protocols be designed modularly.\n\nThe price is setup. Canetti and Fischlin showed that commitments, and with them most interesting tasks, are impossible in the plain UC model, so constructions assume a common reference string or similar trusted setup."},
rewind:{i:"(<i>a</i>, <i>c</i>, <i>z</i>) and (<i>a</i>, <i>c</i>′, <i>z</i>′) accepting with <i>c</i> ≠ <i>c</i>′  ⟹  witness",
t:"Simulators and knowledge extractors often run the adversary, rewind it to an earlier state, and run it again with different randomness. For [[sigma|sigma protocols]], two accepting transcripts with the same first message reveal the witness, a property called special soundness, and the {{Forking lemma|forking lemma}} of Pointcheval and Stern carries this over to Fiat–Shamir signatures in the [[rom|random oracle model]].\n\nRewinding is exactly what breaks against [[quantadv|quantum adversaries]], since measuring a quantum state disturbs it. Watrous showed how to rewind for zero-knowledge, Unruh did so for proofs of knowledge, and Chiesa, Ma, Spooner and Zhandry extended it to succinct arguments."},
blackbox:{i:"one-way functions ⇏<sub>bb</sub> key agreement",
t:"A black-box separation shows that one primitive cannot be built from another when the construction and its proof use the primitive only as an oracle. Impagliazzo and Rudich separated [[kex|key agreement]] from [[owf|one-way functions]], Simon separated [[crhf|collision resistance]] from them, and Reingold, Trevisan and Vadhan mapped the different notions of reducibility. Gentry and Wichs showed that adaptively sound [[snark|SNARGs]] for NP cannot be proven secure from any falsifiable assumption via black-box reductions.\n\nThese results draw the walls between Minicrypt and Cryptomania. Non-black-box techniques can climb them: Barak's zero-knowledge argument uses the adversary's code, and [[snarg|SNARGs for P]] avoid the Gentry–Wichs barrier by restricting the statements proved."},
ggm:{i:"every generic algorithm for discrete log in a group of prime order <i>p</i> needs Ω(√<i>p</i>) group operations",
t:"A generic algorithm treats group elements as labels with no meaning. It can multiply two elements, invert one and test whether two are equal, but it cannot use anything about how elements are represented. Shoup modelled this with random encodings, and Maurer with handles to an abstract group. Shoup proved that any generic algorithm for [[dlog|discrete log]] in a group of prime order p needs about √p operations, which matches {{Baby-step giant-step|baby-step giant-step}} and Pollard's rho. The same bound holds for CDH and DDH.\n\nThe bound matters because, on carefully chosen {{Elliptic curve|elliptic curves}}, nobody knows an attack that beats the generic ones. That has made the model the standard sanity check for new assumptions, especially the many variants built on [[pairings|bilinear maps]], and Groth16 [[snark|SNARKs]] are proven secure in it.\n\nLike the [[rom|random oracle model]], it is a heuristic. Dent built schemes that are secure in the generic group model but broken in every real group. Fuchsbauer, Kiltz and Loss proposed the algebraic group model as a middle ground: the adversary can look at representations, but every group element it outputs must come with an explanation as a combination of elements it was given. Proofs in that model become [[reduce|reductions]] to concrete assumptions."},
quantadv:{i:"no unitary <i>U</i> satisfies <i>U</i>|<i>ψ</i>⟩|0⟩ = |<i>ψ</i>⟩|<i>ψ</i>⟩ for every |<i>ψ</i>⟩",
t:"A quantum adversary threatens cryptography twice. {{Shor's algorithm}} breaks [[factoring]] and [[dlog|discrete log]] outright, which motivates {{Post-quantum cryptography|post-quantum cryptography}}. Less obviously, classical proofs can fail even when the assumption survives: the {{No-cloning theorem|no-cloning theorem}} forbids copying the adversary's state, so [[rewind|rewinding]] and recording [[rom|random oracle]] queries stop working.\n\nBoneh and coauthors formalized the [[qrom|quantum random oracle model]], and Zhandry's compressed oracles restored many classical arguments. {{Grover's algorithm}} roughly halves the bit security of symmetric keys, which is why 256-bit keys are recommended for long-term use."},
qrom:{i:"|<i>x</i>, <i>y</i>⟩ ↦ |<i>x</i>, <i>y</i> ⊕ <i>H</i>(<i>x</i>)⟩",
t:"A hash function is public code, so a [[quantadv|quantum adversary]] can run it on a superposition of inputs. Boneh, Dagdelen, Fischlin, Lehmann, Schaffner and Zhandry argued that the [[rom|random oracle]] must then answer quantum queries, as above. That breaks the three classical tricks: a reduction cannot look at a query without disturbing it, cannot keep a transcript of the adversary's queries, and cannot simply reprogram the oracle at one point.\n\nZhandry's compressed oracles repair recording. Purify the random function and view it in the Fourier basis, and the oracle's state tracks a small database of the points queried so far, which a reduction can inspect much like a classical transcript. Measure-and-reprogram lemmas, due to Don, Fehr, Majenz and Schaffner, repair programming, and with them [[sigma|Fiat–Shamir]] signatures, ML-DSA among them, have proofs in the QROM. The Fujisaki–Okamoto transform, which builds [[cca|CCA-secure]] key encapsulation from weaker encryption and is how ML-KEM gets its security, also has QROM proofs, though with looser bounds.\n\nNot everything carries over. Proofs that rewind the adversary as well as program the oracle need the separate [[rewind|quantum rewinding]] techniques as well."},
ip:{i:"IP = PSPACE",
t:"An {{Interactive proof system|interactive proof}} lets an all-powerful prover convince a randomized polynomial-time verifier through rounds of messages. Goldwasser, Micali and Rackoff, and Babai with {{Arthur–Merlin protocol|Arthur–Merlin games}}, introduced the model in 1985.\n\nIts power was settled by Lund, Fortnow, Karloff and Nisan and by Shamir: {{IP (complexity)|IP}} equals {{PSPACE}}. The key tool is arithmetization together with the sum-check protocol, built on [[algebra|low-degree polynomials]]. Goldwasser, Kalai and Rothblum later made the prover efficient for low-depth circuits, the start of doubly efficient proofs and practical delegation."},
zk:{i:"View<sub><i>V</i>*</sub>⟨<i>P</i>(<i>w</i>), <i>V</i>*⟩(<i>x</i>) ≈<sub>c</sub> <i>S</i>(<i>x</i>)   for every <i>x</i> ∈ <i>L</i>",
t:"A {{Zero-knowledge proof|zero-knowledge proof}} convinces a verifier that a statement is true and reveals nothing else: whatever the verifier sees, a [[simul|simulator]] could have produced without the witness. Goldreich, Micali and Wigderson showed that every NP statement has one if [[owf|one-way functions]] exist, by committing to a {{Graph coloring|3-coloring}} of a graph and opening one random edge at a time.\n\nStatistical zero-knowledge has its own complexity theory, including a complete problem found by Sahai and Vadhan, and Ostrovsky and Wigderson showed that non-trivial zero-knowledge implies a form of one-way function. The non-interactive version is [[nizk|NIZK]], and the succinct one is a [[snark|zk-SNARK]]. [[mpcith|MPC-in-the-head]] builds zero-knowledge proofs out of secure computation protocols."},
mpcith:{i:"commit to views of <i>n</i> parties computing <i>R</i>(<i>x</i>, <i>w</i>),  open <i>t</i>",
t:"Ishai, Kushilevitz, Ostrovsky and Sahai observed that a [[mpc|secure computation]] protocol can be turned into a [[zk|zero-knowledge proof]]. The prover secret-shares the witness w among n imaginary parties, runs a protocol computing the relation R(x, w) entirely in their own head, and [[commit|commits]] to each party's view. The verifier asks to see a few views and checks that they agree with each other and end in acceptance. Privacy of the MPC against the opened parties gives zero-knowledge; a cheating prover must leave some pair of views inconsistent, which the verifier catches with noticeable probability, and repetition drives the error down.\n\nThe transformation uses the primitives inside the MPC only as black boxes, and even [[mpcit|honest-majority protocols]] with information-theoretic security give efficient proofs. ZKBoo made the idea practical for Boolean circuits, and the Picnic signature scheme applied Fiat–Shamir to a proof of knowledge of a block-cipher key: a [[sigs|signature]] whose security rests only on [[prp|symmetric primitives]] and hashing, and so is plausibly post-quantum. Several candidates in NIST's additional signature round follow this design. Ligero combined the idea with Reed–Solomon codes to get proofs about the square root of the circuit size."},
sigma:{i:"<i>a</i> = <i>g</i><sup><i>r</i></sup>,   <i>z</i> = <i>r</i> + <i>cx</i>,   accept iff <i>g</i><sup><i>z</i></sup> = <i>a</i>·<i>y</i><sup><i>c</i></sup>   (Schnorr)",
t:"A sigma protocol is a three-move {{Proof of knowledge|proof of knowledge}}: commit, receive a random challenge, respond. Schnorr's protocol for knowing a [[dlog|discrete log]] is the model case, and Cramer, Damgård and Schoenmakers showed how to combine sigma protocols into proofs of OR-statements.\n\nThe {{Fiat–Shamir heuristic}} removes the interaction by replacing the challenge with a hash of the transcript, which turns Schnorr's protocol into {{Schnorr signature|Schnorr signatures}}. It is sound in the [[rom|random oracle model]]. Proving it sound for concrete hash functions, through correlation intractability from [[lattices|LWE]], is a major recent result (Canetti and coauthors; Peikert and Shiehian)."},
nizk:{i:"<i>π</i> ← <i>P</i>(crs, <i>x</i>, <i>w</i>),   <i>V</i>(crs, <i>x</i>, <i>π</i>) accepts, and <i>π</i> hides <i>w</i>",
t:"A {{Non-interactive zero-knowledge proof|non-interactive zero-knowledge proof}} is a single message, checked against a common reference string. Blum, Feldman and Micali introduced the notion; Feige, Lapidot and Shamir built NIZKs for NP from [[tdf|trapdoor permutations]], and Groth, Ostrovsky and Sahai gave perfect NIZKs from [[pairings]].\n\nFor years lattices were the missing case, until Peikert and Shiehian obtained NIZK for NP from plain [[lattices|LWE]], using the correlation-intractable hashing behind sound [[sigma|Fiat–Shamir]]. NIZKs in turn give [[cca|CCA security]] through the Naor–Yung paradigm."},
pcp:{i:"NP = PCP[<i>O</i>(log <i>n</i>), <i>O</i>(1)]",
t:"A {{Probabilistically checkable proof|probabilistically checkable proof}} can be verified by reading a constant number of its bits. The {{PCP theorem}}, due to Arora and Safra and to Arora, Lund, Motwani, Sudan and Szegedy, says every NP statement has one; it builds on the result of Babai, Fortnow and Lund that two-prover interactive proofs capture {{NEXPTIME}}. Dinur later gave a combinatorial proof by gap amplification on [[expanders|expander graphs]].\n\nThe theorem is equivalent to central results in {{Hardness of approximation|hardness of approximation}}, and it is the root of succinct arguments: Kilian committed to a PCP with a [[crhf|Merkle tree]], and Micali made the protocol non-interactive in the [[rom|random oracle model]]."},
iop:{i:"IOP + random oracle  ⟹  SNARG   (Ben-Sasson, Chiesa, Spooner)",
t:"An interactive oracle proof is a multi-round [[pcp|PCP]]: in each round the prover sends a long message, and the verifier queries a few positions of it before replying. Introduced by Ben-Sasson, Chiesa and Spooner, and independently by Reingold, Rothblum and Rothblum, IOPs are far easier to make efficient than PCPs.\n\nThe BCS compiler turns any IOP into a [[snark|SNARK]] by committing to each message with a [[crhf|Merkle tree]] and deriving the challenges by hashing. The FRI protocol, which tests proximity to {{Reed–Solomon error correction|Reed–Solomon}} codes, and polynomial IOPs paired with polynomial commitments are the templates behind most deployed proof systems."},
snark:{i:"|<i>π</i>| = poly(<i>λ</i>, log |<i>C</i>|),   verification ≪ |<i>C</i>|",
t:"A SNARK, a succinct non-interactive argument of knowledge, proves an NP statement with a proof far shorter than the witness and a verifier far faster than recomputing. Micali's CS proofs achieved this in the [[rom|random oracle model]]; the quadratic arithmetic programs of Gennaro, Gentry, Parno and Raykova led to Groth16, whose proofs are three group elements on a [[pairings|pairing-friendly]] curve. The zk-SNARK section of the {{Non-interactive zero-knowledge proof|non-interactive zero-knowledge}} article surveys the landscape.\n\nEvery known construction relies on random oracles, knowledge assumptions or something similar, and Gentry and Wichs explained why: adaptively sound SNARGs for NP cannot be proven from falsifiable assumptions by [[blackbox|black-box reductions]]. Most practical SNARKs now pair a polynomial IOP with a [[polycommit|polynomial commitment]]. Recursion and [[folding]] build incrementally verifiable computation on top."},
polycommit:{i:"<i>e</i>(<i>C</i> − [<i>y</i>]<sub>1</sub>, [1]<sub>2</sub>) = <i>e</i>(<i>π</i>, [<i>τ</i> − <i>z</i>]<sub>2</sub>)   ⟺   <i>f</i>(<i>z</i>) = <i>y</i>   (KZG)",
t:"A polynomial commitment binds the prover to a polynomial f with a short string, after which the prover can convince anyone that f(z) = y at any requested point z. In the scheme of Kate, Zaverucha and Goldberg the commitment is [f(τ)]<sub>1</sub> for a secret τ buried in a trusted setup, and an opening is a commitment to the quotient (f(X) − y)/(X − z); one [[pairings|pairing]] check, as above, confirms the division. Openings are a single group element, but the setup must be generated honestly and the scheme falls to quantum computers.\n\nTwo transparent families avoid the setup. FRI commits to the evaluations of f in a [[crhf|Merkle tree]] and tests that they are close to a low-degree {{Reed–Solomon error correction|Reed–Solomon codeword}}. It needs only hashing, so it is plausibly post-quantum, and it underlies STARKs. Inner-product arguments, as in Bulletproofs, rest on [[dlog|discrete log]] and have logarithmic proofs but linear-time verification.\n\nThe modern SNARK recipe is modular: design an information-theoretic [[iop|polynomial IOP]], in which the verifier queries polynomials at points of its choice, then replace each oracle with a polynomial commitment and apply Fiat–Shamir. Plonk and Marlin follow this recipe, and the {{Schwartz–Zippel lemma}} from [[algebra]] is why a few random evaluations are convincing. Deferring openings instead of checking them, as Halo does, is the starting point of [[folding|accumulation and folding]]."},
snarg:{i:"|<i>π</i>| = poly(<i>λ</i>, log <i>T</i>) for a <i>T</i>-step computation, from LWE",
t:"For deterministic computations, and for batches of NP statements, succinct arguments can be built from standard assumptions, sidestepping the [[blackbox|Gentry–Wichs barrier]] that applies to all of NP. Kalai, Raz and Rothblum built delegation from [[pir|private information retrieval]] and no-signaling PCPs, and Kalai, Paneth and Yang made it publicly verifiable from [[pairings|bilinear maps]].\n\nChoudhuri, Jain and Jin gave SNARGs for P and batch arguments for NP from [[lattices|LWE]]. Batch arguments have since become a basic building block for further delegation and succinct-proof results."},
folding:{i:"(<i>x</i><sub>1</sub>, <i>w</i><sub>1</sub>), (<i>x</i><sub>2</sub>, <i>w</i><sub>2</sub>) ⟼ (<i>x</i><sub>1</sub> + <i>r</i>·<i>x</i><sub>2</sub>, <i>w</i><sub>1</sub> + <i>r</i>·<i>w</i><sub>2</sub>),   schematically",
t:"Incrementally verifiable computation, introduced by Valiant, proves a long computation step by step so that one short proof covers everything so far. Recursive [[snark|SNARKs]] do it by proving the verifier inside the next proof, and accumulation schemes (Bünz, Chiesa, Mishra and Spooner) postpone most of that work.\n\nFolding schemes go further. Nova folds two instances of a relaxed constraint system into one with a random linear combination, using homomorphic [[dlog|Pedersen-style]] commitments, so each step costs little more than a few group operations. LatticeFold, by Boneh and Chen, brings folding to [[lattices]], aiming at post-quantum security over small fields."},
mpc:{i:"REAL<sub><i>π</i></sub>(<i>x</i><sub>1</sub>, …, <i>x</i><sub><i>n</i></sub>) ≈ IDEAL<sub><i>f</i></sub>(<i>x</i><sub>1</sub>, …, <i>x</i><sub><i>n</i></sub>)",
t:"{{Secure multi-party computation}} lets mutually distrustful parties compute a joint function of their inputs while learning nothing beyond the output, as if a trusted party had done it, even when most of them are dishonest. {{Yao's Millionaires' problem}} started the area. Yao's [[garbled|garbled circuits]] solve the two-party case, and the compiler of Goldreich, Micali and Wigderson the multiparty one.\n\nKilian showed that [[ot|oblivious transfer]] suffices, and [[simul|simulation]] is how security is defined and proven. Without an honest majority, fairness is impossible in general; with one, [[mpcit|information-theoretic protocols]] exist. Practical protocols such as SPDZ pair an expensive preprocessing phase with a fast information-theoretic online phase."},
garbled:{i:"(<i>F̃</i>, <i>X̃</i>) reveals <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) and nothing more",
t:"A {{Garbled circuit|garbled circuit}} encrypts a Boolean circuit gate by gate, replacing each wire value with a random label, so that whoever holds the labels for one input can evaluate the circuit and learn only its output. Yao used it for two-party [[mpc|secure computation]]: one party garbles, and the other obtains the labels for its input by [[ot|oblivious transfer]].\n\nBellare, Hoang and Rogaway formalized garbling schemes, and free-XOR and half-gates made them fast. The idea generalizes to randomized encodings, which Applebaum, Ishai and Kushilevitz used to show that much of cryptography can be computed in constant depth (NC⁰). Reusable garbled circuits lead to [[fe|functional encryption]]."},
fhe:{i:"Dec(Eval(<i>f</i>, Enc(<i>m</i><sub>1</sub>), …, Enc(<i>m</i><sub><i>k</i></sub>))) = <i>f</i>(<i>m</i><sub>1</sub>, …, <i>m</i><sub><i>k</i></sub>)",
t:"Fully {{Homomorphic encryption|homomorphic encryption}} allows arbitrary computation on ciphertexts. Every known scheme adds noise that grows with each operation. Gentry's 2009 insight, bootstrapping, refreshes a ciphertext by homomorphically evaluating the decryption circuit, which requires the scheme to stay secure when it encrypts its own key, an assumption called circular security.\n\nBrakerski and Vaikuntanathan based FHE on standard [[lattices|LWE]]. BGV and BFV, built on [[rlwe|Ring-LWE]], support leveled computation on packed integers, GSW gave a simpler matrix-based scheme, and CKKS handles approximate arithmetic on real numbers. FHE gives single-server [[pir|PIR]] and appears inside succinct [[fe|functional encryption]]."},
fe:{i:"Dec(sk<sub><i>f</i></sub>, Enc(<i>x</i>)) = <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>)",
t:"{{Functional encryption}} issues secret keys that reveal a chosen function of the plaintext and nothing more. Boneh, Sahai and Waters formalized it, showed that it generalizes [[ibe|identity- and attribute-based encryption]], and proved that simulation-based security is impossible in general.\n\nBounded-collusion schemes exist from standard assumptions: Goldwasser and coauthors built succinct single-key FE from [[lattices|LWE]] using [[fhe|FHE]] and reusable [[garbled|garbled circuits]]. Compact FE, where the ciphertext does not grow with the function, turns out to be as powerful as [[io|indistinguishability obfuscation]] (Ananth and Jain; Bitansky and Vaikuntanathan)."},
io:{i:"<i>C</i><sub>0</sub> ≡ <i>C</i><sub>1</sub>  ⟹  iO(<i>C</i><sub>0</sub>) ≈<sub>c</sub> iO(<i>C</i><sub>1</sub>)",
t:"Obfuscation makes a program unintelligible while preserving its function. Barak and coauthors proved that the ideal notion, virtual black-box obfuscation, is impossible in general. {{Indistinguishability obfuscation}} asks only that obfuscations of functionally equivalent circuits be indistinguishable.\n\nGarg, Gentry, Halevi, Raykova, Sahai and Waters proposed the first candidate in 2013, and Sahai and Waters' punctured programming showed that iO implies [[pke|public-key encryption]], [[nizk|NIZKs]] and much more, making it a central hub. After many candidates were broken, Jain, Lin and Sahai built iO from well-founded assumptions: [[pairings|SXDH on pairing groups]], [[lpn|LPN]] over fields and local pseudorandom generators. Its weaker relative, [[we|witness encryption]], already gives public-key encryption."},
we:{i:"<i>R</i>(<i>x</i>, <i>w</i>) = 1  ⟹  Dec(<i>w</i>, Enc(<i>x</i>, <i>m</i>)) = <i>m</i>",
t:"Ordinary encryption uses a key that someone generated. {{Witness encryption}}, introduced by Garg, Gentry, Sahai and Waters, uses an NP statement instead: anyone who knows a witness that the statement is true can recover the message, and if the statement is false the ciphertext reveals nothing. Their candidate encoded an instance of the NP-complete {{Exact cover|exact cover}} problem in multilinear maps.\n\nIt sits just below [[io|obfuscation]]. An obfuscated program that checks a witness and then outputs the message is a witness encryption, so iO implies it. In the other direction, encrypting to the statement that a string lies in the image of a [[prg|PRG]] gives [[pke|public-key encryption]]: the public key is G(s) and the secret key is s. Security follows because G(s) is indistinguishable from a uniform string, for which the statement is almost surely false.\n\nWhether witness encryption needs the full strength of obfuscation is open. Tsabary, and independently Vaikuntanathan, Wee and Wichs, built candidates directly from [[lattices|lattices]] under evasive LWE, a newer and still-debated assumption about LWE samples published alongside short preimages."},
pir:{i:"client learns <i>D</i>[<i>i</i>],   server learns nothing about <i>i</i>,   communication <i>o</i>(|<i>D</i>|)",
t:"{{Private information retrieval}} lets a client fetch an entry of a database without revealing which one. With several non-colluding servers it can be information-theoretic (Chor, Goldreich, Kushilevitz and Sudan). With a single server it needs assumptions, first [[factoring|quadratic residuosity]] (Kushilevitz and Ostrovsky) and later [[fhe|homomorphic encryption]].\n\nThe server normally has to touch every entry. Preprocessing breaks that barrier: Corrigan-Gibbs and Kogan gave sublinear online time, and Lin, Mook and Wichs built doubly efficient PIR from Ring-LWE, with polylogarithmic server work after preprocessing. PIR is also an ingredient in [[snarg|delegation]], and a close relative of [[oram|oblivious RAM]]."},
oram:{i:"addresses touched by Compile(<i>P</i>) on <i>D</i> ≈ on <i>D</i>′,   overhead Θ(log <i>N</i>)",
t:"Encrypting memory hides what is stored but not where a program looks, and access patterns alone leak a great deal: which record a query touched, which branch a computation took. {{Oblivious RAM}}, introduced by Goldreich and Ostrovsky for software protection, compiles a program so that the sequence of addresses it touches is independent of its data, at a polylogarithmic cost per access. Their constructions used a [[prf|pseudorandom function]] to scatter blocks, with periodic oblivious reshuffling.\n\nPath ORAM made the idea strikingly simple. Blocks live in a binary tree, each mapped to a random leaf; every access reads and rewrites one root-to-leaf path and remaps the block to a fresh leaf. Goldreich and Ostrovsky had shown that Ω(log N) overhead is necessary in a restricted model, Larsen and Nielsen proved it for all online ORAMs, and OptORAMa matched the bound.\n\nORAM is the RAM-model counterpart of [[pir|PIR]]. PIR hides a read from a server holding the database in the clear; ORAM hides every read and write from a server holding encrypted, shuffled data that the client manages. Running [[mpc|secure computation]] over an ORAM lets parties compute on large data in time sublinear in its size, and garbled RAM does the same for [[garbled|garbled circuits]]."},
qcrypto:{i:"BB84 states:  |0⟩, |1⟩, |+⟩, |−⟩",
t:"{{Quantum cryptography}} uses quantum information as a resource. Wiesner's conjugate coding led both to {{Quantum money|quantum money}} and to {{BB84}}, the first {{Quantum key distribution|quantum key distribution}} protocol, whose security rests on physics rather than computational hardness: measuring in the wrong basis disturbs the state. The raw key is then cleaned up by [[privamp|privacy amplification]].\n\nThe {{No-cloning theorem|no-cloning theorem}} enables unclonable cryptography, such as quantum money, copy-protected software and unclonable encryption. Ji, Liu and Song introduced pseudorandom quantum states, and Kretschmer showed they may exist even if [[owf|one-way functions]] do not, opening a world below Minicrypt."},
derand:{i:"E ⊄ SIZE(2<sup><i>εn</i></sup>)  ⟹  P = BPP",
t:"Derandomization asks whether randomness really helps efficient computation. Nisan and Wigderson showed that hard functions yield pseudorandom generators good enough to replace randomness, and Impagliazzo and Wigderson that if exponential time requires exponential-size circuits, then P = {{BPP (complexity)|BPP}}.\n\nThe definition matches cryptography's [[prg|PRGs]], but the trade differs. Derandomization generators may run longer than the circuits they fool and need only [[circuits|circuit lower bounds]], while cryptographic PRGs must be efficient and need [[owf|one-way functions]]."},
circuits:{i:"PRFs computable in a class 𝒞  ⟹  no natural proof of lower bounds against 𝒞",
t:"{{Circuit complexity}} seeks explicit functions that require large circuits. Razborov and Rudich's [[natural|natural proofs]] barrier shows that most known lower-bound techniques, if they worked against a class, would also break every [[prf|pseudorandom function]] computable in that class.\n\nSince the {{Naor–Reingold pseudorandom function}} is believed secure and lies in {{TC0}}, natural proofs are unlikely to give lower bounds against TC⁰. Lower bounds in turn feed [[derand|derandomization]], and the circuit minimization problem is the starting point of [[metac|meta-complexity]]."},
expanders:{i:"max(|<i>λ</i><sub>2</sub>|, |<i>λ</i><sub><i>n</i></sub>|) ≤ <i>λ</i> &lt; <i>d</i>",
t:"An {{Expander graph|expander}} is a sparse graph that is nonetheless highly connected. The spectral gap between the degree d and the next largest eigenvalue measures how fast random walks mix, and walks on expanders give randomness-efficient sampling.\n\nIn cryptography, Goldreich, Impagliazzo, Levin, Venkatesan and Zuckerman used expanders to amplify weak [[owf|one-way functions]] without blowing up the input length. Dinur's combinatorial proof of the [[pcp|PCP theorem]] amplifies the gap of a constraint graph by expander-based powering."},
algebra:{i:"Pr<sub><i>r</i>∈<i>S</i><sup><i>n</i></sup></sub>[<i>p</i>(<i>r</i>) = 0] ≤ <i>d</i>/|<i>S</i>|   for nonzero <i>p</i> of degree <i>d</i>",
t:"Low-degree polynomials over finite fields run through the whole subject. The {{Schwartz–Zippel lemma}} bounds how often a nonzero polynomial vanishes at a random point, which underlies {{Polynomial identity testing|polynomial identity testing}}, the sum-check protocol and arithmetization in [[ip|interactive proofs]].\n\nThe same polynomials give [[ss|Shamir sharing]], [[hashing|t-wise independent hashing]] and {{Reed–Solomon error correction|Reed–Solomon codes}}, and low-degree testing (Rubinfeld and Sudan) is a pillar of the [[pcp|PCP theorem]]. Modern [[snark|SNARKs]] are largely polynomial arithmetic."},
coding:{i:"<i>m</i> ↦ (<i>p</i><sub><i>m</i></sub>(<i>α</i><sub>1</sub>), …, <i>p</i><sub><i>m</i></sub>(<i>α</i><sub><i>n</i></sub>)),   deg <i>p</i><sub><i>m</i></sub> &lt; <i>k</i>   (Reed–Solomon)",
t:"{{Coding theory}} appears all over cryptography. [[ss|Shamir shares]] form a {{Reed–Solomon error correction|Reed–Solomon}} codeword; the [[hardcore|Goldreich–Levin theorem]] is {{List decoding|list decoding}} of the {{Hadamard code}}; [[lpn|LPN]] and the McEliece cryptosystem are decoding problems for random and {{Goppa code|Goppa}} codes; and FRI in [[iop|interactive oracle proofs]] tests proximity to Reed–Solomon codes.\n\nThe traffic runs both ways. Sudan's list-decoding algorithm for Reed–Solomon codes, and the use of list decoding by Sudan, Trevisan and Vadhan to build pseudorandom generators, brought cryptographic ideas back into coding and complexity."},
learning:{i:"cryptography exists  ⟹  simple concept classes are hard to PAC-learn",
t:"{{Computational learning theory}} and cryptography are two sides of one coin. Valiant's {{Probably approximately correct learning|PAC model}} asks for efficient learners, and Kearns and Valiant showed that cryptographic hardness makes classes such as Boolean formulae and finite automata hard to learn.\n\nIn the other direction, learning problems are themselves cryptographic assumptions: [[lattices|LWE]] is learning noisy linear functions mod q, and [[lpn|LPN]] is learning noisy parities. Blum, Furst, Kearns and Lipton made this connection explicit."},
geometry:{i:"‖<i>b</i><sub>1</sub>‖ ≤ 2<sup>(<i>n</i>−1)/2</sup> <i>λ</i><sub>1</sub>(<i>L</i>)   (LLL)",
t:"The geometry of numbers studies {{Lattice (group)|lattices}} and their short vectors. The {{Lenstra–Lenstra–Lovász lattice basis reduction algorithm|LLL algorithm}} finds, in polynomial time, a vector within an exponential factor of the shortest, and it broke early knapsack-based cryptosystems; block reduction (BKZ) trades running time for better factors.\n\nOn the hardness side, Ajtai showed that the {{Lattice problem|shortest vector problem}} is NP-hard under randomized reductions, while Aharonov and Regev placed √n-approximation in NP ∩ coNP. [[lattices|Lattice cryptography]] lives in the gap: polynomial approximation factors, believed hard yet unlikely to be NP-hard. Micciancio and Goldwasser's book Complexity of Lattice Problems covers the area."},
quantcomp:{i:"factoring, discrete log ∈ BQP",
t:"{{Quantum computing|Quantum computers}} set the threat model for modern cryptography. {{Shor's algorithm}} factors integers and computes discrete logarithms in polynomial time, placing both in {{BQP}} and breaking RSA, Diffie–Hellman and elliptic-curve cryptography. {{Grover's algorithm}} gives only a quadratic speedup for search, which symmetric schemes absorb with longer keys.\n\nQuantum ideas also supply evidence: Regev's proof that [[lattices|LWE]] is as hard as worst-case lattice problems uses a quantum reduction. And quantum information is itself a resource, for [[qcrypto|quantum cryptography]]."}
};

